            "content": "---\ntype: Research\ntitle: \"Decision-model limits and request layout for ranking\"\ndescription: \"Per-model context ceilings, billing and overflow behavior across Jev, Liquid d1, Gemini Decision 1, GPT-6 Luna Decisions and Laya; published evidence on candidate layout for decision-model reranking; and the configuration and lane decisions taken from it.\"\ntags: [decision-models, system-one, jev, laya, d1, gemini, rerank, limits]\nstatus: draft\ngenerated: { by: \"claude-code/claude-opus-5-5\", at: \"2026-11-13\" }\n---\n\n# Decision-model limits and request layout for ranking\n\nDraft record. Sources are cited inline as URLs read on 2026-11-13 through a\nsummarizing fetcher; quotes are as relayed, not raw page text. Convert to\nfootnoted `sources` before publishing.\n\n## Question\n\n`laya-multilingual`, served by the WebPros LM Panel, was to become a decision\nmodel for `grep` and `web_search` ranking. Its checkpoints accept 512 to 1,024\ntokens per question. The current lane packs up to 256 cards into one shared\nstate (`DECISION_MAX_CARDS_PER_REQUEST`). What must change in the configuration\nmodel and the lane, and is the shared-state layout itself correct?\n\n## Live probe: Laya on the LM Panel gateway\n\n- The gateway serves `laya-english` and `laya-multilingual` (LiteLLM mode\n  `decisions`, `typesafe/english` and `typesafe/multilingual` behind\n  `laya-serve:8000`) on `POST {baseUrl}/systemone`. The existing System One\n  codec accepts the response; the served `model` is `laya-rl-agent` for both,\n  with the checkpoint only in `routing.model`.\n- The gateway certificate chains to an internal Puppet root CA; Node needs\n  `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` pointing at it.\n- Real grep cards (objective about RLS tenant isolation, `tenant` matches in\n  `apps/api/src`, built through `buildJudgeRequest`), `laya-multilingual`:\n\n| Cards | Input tokens | State tokens | Truncated     | Latency | Probability range |\n| ----- | ------------ | ------------ | ------------- | ------- | ----------------- |\n| 1     | 251          | 153          | no            | 0.73 s  | 0.336             |\n| 4     | 1,624        | 308          | no            | 0.96 s  | 0.325-0.396       |\n| 8     | 5,368        | 573          | no            | 3.1 s   | 0.411-0.429       |\n| 16    | 16,384       | 1,030        | all questions | 11.7 s  | 0.599-0.624       |\n| 32    | HTTP 500     |              |               | 26.9 s  |                   |\n\n- Input tokens grow as questions x state: the server re-encodes the state per\n  question. At 16 cards it truncated the state, still returned HTTP 200, and\n  answers collapsed to one value; the current codec would accept them.\n- After the 32-card request (about 20 KB) `laya-serve` refused connections for\n  both checkpoints and had not recovered at the end of the session. Causation\n  is likely but unproven without server logs.\n- A first two-question toy probe answered both questions inverted on\n  multilingual (RLS file 0.21, CSS file 0.66) and correctly on english.\n\n## Member limits\n\n| Member              | Wire                        | State + one question            | State + all questions                  | Questions | State billed            | Overflow                     |\n| ------------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | --------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------- |\n| Jev (TypeSafe)      | `/v1/systemone`             | 32k                             | 64k (262k with long-context header)    | no limit  | once                    | 400 `max_tokens_exceeded`    |\n| Jev via OpenRouter  | `/api/v1/systemone`         | not stated                      | 262k; long-context mode above ~32 KB   | not found | not stated              | not found                    |\n| Liquid d1, d1.1     | `/decisions/v1/systemone`   | 65,536 (d1-ultra 32,768), 250 KB text | not found                        | 128       | once (docs conflict)    | 422                          |\n| Gemini Decision 1   | `/v1beta/decisions`         | no separate limit               | 262,144                                | 1,000     | once                    | 400                          |\n| GPT-6 Luna Decisions| `/v1/decisions`             | not documented                  | not documented                         | 400       | not stated              | not documented               |\n| Laya multilingual   | `/v1/systemone` (laya serve)| 1,024 (256 for the question)    | server `LAYA_MAX_TOKEN_BUDGET`         | not found | per question (measured) | truncates, `usage.truncated` |\n| Laya english        | same                        | 512 (192 for the question)      | same                                   | not found | per question (inferred) | same                         |\n\nSources: https://docs.typesafe.ai/models.md, https://docs.typesafe.ai/api/errors.md,\nhttps://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/community/jev.md,\nhttps://docs.liquid.ai/api/reference/decision-models/make-decisions,\nhttps://aistudio.google.com/docs/decisions-api,\nhttps://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/decisions.md,\nhttps://huggingface.co/convaiinnovations/laya-multilingual, https://pypi.org/project/laya/.\n\nLaya quality: the vendor card puts zero-shot typed-decision accuracy at 0.362\n(`laya`) and 0.352 (`laya-multilingual`) against a 0.318 random baseline, and\n0.766 for the separate `laya-typed-decisions` checkpoint (Jev 0.727). The\ngateway serves only the base checkpoints. `LAYA_JEV_STRICT=1` removes the\ntruncation fields.\n\nJev billing in llame's own fixtures matches state-once: 40 cards cost\n$0.000284, about 6.7k tokens at $0.042 per million.\n\n## Candidate layout evidence\n\nNo source compares one candidate per request with many candidates in one state\non the same workload.\n\n| Source                                                                                            | Layout                                         | Measured                                                                               |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Unblocked, https://getunblocked.com/blog/jev-in-production-vs-cross-encoder/                      | shared state, 20 per request, shuffled         | beat incumbent cross-encoder; per-pair variant below it (nDCG@5 -0.033)                |\n| Opine, https://tryopine.com/blog/jev-as-reranker                                                  | shared state, 20-30 per request, parallel      | 64 LLM-graded queries: nDCG@20 0.876 at 200 candidates, Voyage 3 0.815, Luna 0.864     |\n| TypeSafe rerank and RAG cookbooks, https://docs.typesafe.ai/cookbooks/rerank_typesafe.md          | one candidate per request                      | vendor top-k gains; no layout comparison                                               |\n| TypeSafe infinite cardinality, https://docs.typesafe.ai/cookbooks/infinite_cardinality_choice.md  | shared state, 300 per request, then a choice   | vendor 78% top-1                                                                       |\n| Hindsight, https://hindsight.vectorize.io/blog/2026/09/24/adding-jev-reranker-what-we-learned     | one choice over candidates                     | recall@1 0.94 vs 0.87 per candidate; scores are relative; \"none\" option backfired      |\n| TypeSafe jaggedness, https://docs.typesafe.ai/model-jaggedness.md                                 | -                                              | accuracy falls with unrelated state; indirection costs accuracy; choice order bias     |\n| Liquid decision guide, https://docs.liquid.ai/guides/decision-model-guide.md                      | one chunk per call, threshold 0.5              | none                                                                                   |\n| Injection against LLM rankers, https://arxiv.org/html/2602.16752                                  | -                                              | 99-100% attack success when candidates share a context                                 |\n\n## Decisions\n\nTaken with Leo on 2026-11-13. Codes are the review-round identifiers.\n\n- **G1** Limits live on each `decisionModels[]` entry: optional\n  `limits.perQuestionContextTokens`, `limits.perRequestContextTokens`,\n  `limits.maxQuestionsPerRequest`.\n- **G2 / G9** Omitted limits default to 8,192 per question and 32,768 per\n  request, with no question default.\n- **G3** Units are tokens, matching vendor documentation.\n- **G4** `maxConcurrentRequests` on the `system-one` provider entry, default 4,\n  shared by every catalogue entry on that provider.\n- **G5** A response reporting `usage.truncated` or `state_tokens_dropped > 0`\n  makes its whole request unavailable. Declared limits are the guard; the flag\n  is a backstop.\n- **G6** First change: limits, concurrency, packing and truncation only. Gemini\n  and OpenAI provider types are later proposals.\n- **G7** Keep the shared-state layout, capped at 32 cards per request by a lane\n  constant, and lower where the declared limits require, down to one card.\n- **G8** A card that alone exceeds the window is cut to fit with the existing\n  code-point cut.\n- **G10** Token estimate: UTF-8 bytes / 3, rounded up, over state and question\n  text.\n- **G11** No per-tool item cap; the tool deadline and provider concurrency bound\n  a call.\n- **G12** Production keeps canonical card order; the eval records each case in\n  two orders to measure position effects. Future idea, low confidence: a\n  deterministic code-aware first pass for `grep` (prefer usages, assignments,\n  declarations over imports), related to #1606 and #801.\n- **G13 + G15** A separate issue: before the proposal, evaluate Jev on the four\n  `fixtures/ranking` cases with the 32-card shared state against one card per\n  request, in two card orders, plus a cross-card injection test (one card\n  claiming it alone is relevant must not move the other cards beyond an agreed\n  tolerance; if it does, `web_search` moves to one card per request).\n- **G14** A choice pass over the top candidates is research first.\n\n## Open items\n\n- `laya-serve` is down; the LM Panel must restart it, set\n  `LAYA_MAX_TOKEN_BUDGET` / `LAYA_MAX_BATCH_TOKENS`, and serve\n  `laya-typed-decisions` before Laya is evaluated.\n- One card per state versus shared state on Laya: eight requests once the\n  server is back.\n- Liquid's billing pages disagree on whether images are billed once or per\n  question.\n",
