            "content": "## Problem\n\nThe decision lane packs up to 256 cards into one System One request (`DECISION_MAX_CARDS_PER_REQUEST`), whatever the model behind the catalogue entry can accept, and sends every split request at once.\n\n- At full size that request is roughly 30k tokens of state and 60k tokens in total, at or over Jev's documented 32k per-question and 64k per-request ceilings, and above the ~32 KB at which OpenRouter switches Jev to its slower, less accurate long-context mode.\n- Small self-hosted members cannot take it at all. `laya-multilingual` (WebPros LM Panel) accepts 1,024 tokens per question and re-encodes the state for each question. At 16 grep cards it truncated the state, still returned HTTP 200, and its answers collapsed to one value, which the current codec accepts as a ranking. After a 32-card request the server stopped answering.\n- Nothing bounds concurrent requests to one server.\n\n## Outcome\n\nA `decisionModels[]` entry describes what its model accepts, and the lane derives request sizes from that. Card counts stay a ranking concern of the lane, not a model setting.\n\n- Optional `limits` on each entry: `perQuestionContextTokens`, `perRequestContextTokens`, `maxQuestionsPerRequest`. Omitted values default to 8,192 per question and 32,768 per request.\n- Optional `maxConcurrentRequests` on a `system-one` provider, default 4, shared by every entry on that provider.\n- The shared-state layout stays, capped at 32 cards per request and lower when the declared limits require it, down to one card; a card that alone exceeds the window is cut to fit. Tokens are estimated as UTF-8 bytes / 3.\n- A response that reports truncation (`usage.truncated` or `state_tokens_dropped > 0`) makes its request unavailable.\n\nOut of scope: Gemini and OpenAI decision wires, a per-tool item cap, a `choice` pass over top candidates.\n\n## Evidence\n\nA Jev 1.14 eval on the four `fixtures/ranking` cases (shared state of 32 against one card per request, card order reversed, and a cross-card injection case) goes into the proposal's design. Shared state ranked at least as well for about a third of the tokens.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- [ ] `instance-config` accepts and validates the new fields; invalid values fail startup naming the path.\n- [ ] Requests never exceed the declared or default limits, the 32-card cap, or the provider's concurrency.\n- [ ] A truncation report makes the request unavailable.\n- [ ] Operator documentation (`docs/product/operator/decisions.md`) describes the fields and defaults.\n",
