            "content": "## Concern\n\nProposes per-model limits for decision models and a bounded decision lane. Today the lane packs up to 256 cards into one System One request, whatever the model accepts, and sends every split request at once. That size is near Jev's documented ceilings, and it breaks small self-hosted members: `laya-multilingual` truncated silently at 16 cards and stopped answering after a 32-card request.\n\n```diff\n decisionModels:\n   - id: laya-multilingual\n     provider: lmpanel-decisions\n     model: laya-multilingual\n+    limits:                          # all optional; defaults 8,192 / 32,768\n+      perQuestionContextTokens: 1024 # state + one question\n+      perRequestContextTokens: 8192  # state + every question\n+      maxQuestionsPerRequest: 16\n providers:\n   - id: lmpanel-decisions\n     type: system-one\n+    maxConcurrentRequests: 2         # attempts in flight per process, default 4\n```\n\nThe lane keeps one shared state per request and fills requests in canonical order up to 32 cards and the model's limits. It judges at most 256 cards per call, bounds attempts per provider with abortable FIFO slots, cuts a card that alone exceeds a limit, and fails a request closed when the server reports truncation. The design records the Jev layout eval Leo asked for: a 32-card shared state ranked no worse than one card per request on the four `fixtures/ranking` cases, at about a third of the tokens.\n\n## Issues and stack\n\n- Issues served: Refs #1717\n- Stack position: `master` ← this ← `decision-model-limits/impl` ← `decision-model-limits/finalize`\n\n## Review budget\n\n1,012 added lines, all authored OpenSpec Markdown, against `master`. The two MODIFIED `instance-config` blocks copy 186 canonical lines whole, as OpenSpec requires. No generated churn.\n\n## Decisions to review\n\n- **The limits describe the model, not the tool.** Card counts stay lane constants: 32 per request and 256 per call. Rejected: a model-level card limit, limits on the provider, and a built-in model table.\n- **The defaults suit hosted members, not small ones.** A 1,024-token checkpoint must declare its limits, because 32 grep cards fit the defaults.\n- **The 256-card call bound reverses an earlier \"no per-call cap\" decision.** That decision assumed a deadline leaves an unjudged tail. In fact it fails the whole tool call, and grep can send thousands of cards.\n- **Requests keep the shared-state layout and canonical order.** Production write-ups (Unblocked, Opine) used 20–30 cards per request. Our eval could not separate the layouts on quality because the fixtures saturate.\n- **The token estimate is a heuristic:** UTF-8 bytes of the serialized JSON ÷ 3, rounded up, with no tokenizer. A truncation report is a backstop; the declared limits are the guard.\n- **New cross-call queueing risk.** A grep call can now wait behind another Run's attempts on a shared provider inside its roughly 12 s rank window.\n\n## Commands run\n\n- `pnpm exec openspec validate decision-model-limits --strict`: valid.\n- Pre-commit on each commit: `oxfmt` check, markdownlint, and the markdown link check all passed.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n- Two adversarial review rounds of two reviewers each; findings fixed in `fe298c683` and `bdb848f4d`.\n- The Jev eval ran once on `typesafe/jev-1.14` through OpenRouter; its results are in `design.md`.\n\n## Checklist\n\n### General\n\n- [x] Self-reviewed the parent-relative diff against `REVIEW_GUIDE.md`\n- [x] No debug, scratch, or unrelated code in the diff\n- [x] Delivery Project item added and its Status / Next action set\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
